      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(infra): bound jsonl-socket response buffer to prevent OOM",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\n`requestJsonlSocket` could retain peer-controlled response bytes until a newline, close, or timeout. A buggy or hostile local Unix-socket peer could therefore force unbounded memory growth with a newline-free response, while an oversized completed macOS exec response could exceed the requester's new safety budget after the command had already run.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nThe maintainer rewrite moves the invariant to both correct owners:\n\n- The shared JSONL requester now frames raw byte segments, enforces a 16 MiB limit on every complete or incomplete line before concatenation and parsing, preserves split UTF-8, resets accounting after rejected lines, and lets a valid accepted response win before unrelated trailing bytes.\n- The macOS exec producer caps stdout and stderr before response encoding, preserves completed-command status, keeps valid UTF-8 tails, and emits an explicit truncation marker. This prevents an ambiguous null response from encouraging an unsafe command retry.\n\nThe implementation replaces repeated whole-string concatenation with one bounded framing path; callers retain their existing fail-closed behavior.\n\n## User Impact\n\nLocal socket responses can no longer grow requester memory without bound. Large completed exec commands still return their exit state and bounded output instead of becoming indistinguishable from a transport failure.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Exact PR head: `02d522f7d71cb292ba688f0c856e146e7da86869`.\n- Focused TypeScript proof: 10 JSONL-socket and exec-host tests passed.\n- Real Unix-domain socket negative control on current main: a newline-free 14 MiB stream returned only at the 1,504 ms timeout.\n- Same real socket after the rewrite: a 17 MiB line was rejected in 24 ms.\n- macOS producer proof: 7 `ExecApprovalsSocketAuthTests` passed, including a real command producing 4 MiB of output and worst-case JSON escaping.\n- Targeted SwiftFormat and Oxlint checks passed after exact-CI corrections.\n- Final structured autoreviews after both CI corrections: clean, 0.99 correctness confidence.\n- Screenshots are not useful for this nonvisual memory/transport boundary; proof exercises the real socket and real command-output paths.\n\nThe exact-head CI matrix is green across build, TypeScript, lint, macOS Node, macOS Swift, and all test shards. A final retry is running for the QA smoke harness after two unrelated flaky scenarios failed on different attempts while the prior failure passed.\n",
